- How would you reconstruct the exact L/R sequence from the DP table?
- How does this change if moves include taking from both ends with a coin flip to decide who starts?

## Why This Is Asked
- Tests DP state design that tracks both position and budget (flips).
- Requires reconstruction to return exact indices, not just value.

## Key Concepts
- DP state with dual dimension (i, flips)
- Transition depends on current bit; reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesRun(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: k+1}, () => null));
  let bestLen = 0, bestEnd = 0, bestJ = 0;
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<=k; j++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j] : null;
      } else if (j>0) {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j-1] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j-1] : null;
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = 0; prev[i][j] = null;
      }
      if (dp[i][j] > bestLen) { bestLen = dp[i][j]; bestEnd = i; bestJ = j; }
    }
  }
  const start = bestEnd - bestLen + 2;
  return {bestLen, start, end: bestEnd+1, flips: bestJ};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(k)?
- How would you adapt if flips must happen in blocks?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a DP state into a practical recurrence with non-overlap constraint and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over index i and count t
- Non-overlapping constraint handled by dp[i-2] for taking a domino
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const NEG=-1e15;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=2;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      const v1=dp[i-1][t];
      const v2=dp[i-2][t-1]+A[i-2]+A[i-1];
      if(v2>v1){ dp[i][t]=v2; take[i][t]=true; }
      else dp[i][t]=v1;
    }
  }
  // backtrack
  // ... reconstruct domino starts from i=n, t=k
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How to adapt if dominoes have variable lengths or weights?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model partitioning with a simple per-block objective and reconstruct optimal cuts. It reveals DP formulation, range queries, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP across two dimensions (prefix and blocks)
- Range maximum precomputation or on-the-fly tracking
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCostMax(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  // precompute max for all ranges
  const rangeMax = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let cur = 0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      cur = Math.max(cur, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.min(i,m);j++){
      for(let t=j-1; t<=i-1; t++){
        const val = dp[t][j-1] + rangeMax[t][i-1];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){
          dp[i][j] = val;
          par[i][j] = t;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, j = m;
  while(j>0){
    const t = par[i][j];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = t;
    j--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][m], partitions: cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(n m) time using monotonic queues?  
- How would you adapt if each chapter cost were max(A[t..i-1]) + c*(i-t)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
New DP angle focusing on subarray range gain with exact k partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP partitioning
- Precomputing range max/min
- Reconstruction through pointers
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGainKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = A[l], mx = A[l];
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      gain[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      take[i][t] = take[i-1][t];
      if(t>0){
        for(let l=1;l<=i;l++){
          const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + gain[l-1][i-1];
          if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l-1; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const lminus1 = take[i][t];
    const l = lminus1 + 1;
    res.unshift([l, i]);
    i = lminus1; t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct blocks?
- How would the solution change if subarrays must have at least a fixed length L?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests weighted interval scheduling with an exact k constraint and reconstruction; requires sorting, precomputing p(i), and a 2D DP with backtracking; also considerations for memory optimization.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling
- DP with an extra dimension for exact k selections
- Precomputing p(i) (latest non-overlapping job)
- Space-time tradeoffs; backtracking reconstruction
- Stability under large n and k; edge-case handling when K > possible non-overlapping sets

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonOverlapping(jobs, K) {
  // jobs: {s, e, r}; sort by end time
  jobs.sort((a,b)=> a.e - b.e);
  const n = jobs.length;
  const end = jobs.map(j=> j.e);
  // p(i): last index < i with end[p] < start[i]
  const p = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    let lo=0, hi=i-1, ans=-1;
    while (lo<=hi){
      const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
      if (end[mid] < jobs[i].s){ ans=mid; lo=mid+1; } else hi=mid-1;
    }
    p[i]=ans;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=1; t<=K; t++){
      const without = dp[i-1][t];
      const withTake = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? dp[p[i-1]+1][t-1] : (t-1===0?0: -Infinity)) + jobs[i-1].r;
      if (withTake > without){ dp[i][t] = withTake; take[i][t] = true; } else { dp[i][t] = without; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=K;
  while (i>0 && t>0){
    if (take[i][t]){ res.push(i-1); i = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? p[i-1]+1 : 0); t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return { revenue: dp[n][K], indices: res };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to an "at most K" constraint?
- How can you optimize memory to O(n + K) or O(K) and still reconstruct the solution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming skills for selecting exactly k disjoint subarrays with a per-subarray penalty—a common variant in coding interviews that extends standard maximum subarray problems.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-overlapping constraint**: Subarrays must not intersect, handled by the state machine.
- **Dual-state DP**: Maintaining both global (dp) and local (end) states to track ongoing vs. completed selections.
- **Reconstruction**: Tracing back decisions to recover actual subarray endpoints.
- **Optimization**: Balancing time complexity (O(nk)) against space (full vs. rolling arrays).

## Code Example

```javascript
const solve = (A, k, c) => {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const end = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Not taking a subarray ending at i
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'skip';
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = end[i][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'end';
      }

      // Taking/starting subarray at i
      if (t > 0) {
        const startNew = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c;
        const extend = end[i-1][t] + A[i-1] - c;
        end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction logic would go here
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: [] };
};
```

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(nk)
- **Space:** O(nk) for full DP, O(k) for optimized version

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests advanced tree DP with edge deletions and a non-trivial objective—maximizing the sum of component maxima. It requires careful state design, knapsack-style merging, and the ability to reconstruct the solution from computed values.

## Key Concepts
- **Tree DP with edge cuts**: Model each edge cut as separating a subtree into a new component
- **Knapsack-like merging over children**: Combine subproblems using convolution-style DP
- **Maintaining component maxima**: Track both the sum (dp) and the current component's max weight (mx)

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(n, edges, weights, k) {
    const children = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    for (const [u, v] of edges) children[u - 1].push(v - 1);

    const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const mx = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);

    function dfs(u) {
        dp[u][0] = 0;
        mx[u][0] = weights[u];
        for (const v of children[u]) {
            parent[v] = u;
            dfs(v);
            for (let t = k; t >= 0; t--) {
                const ndp = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                const nmx = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                for (let t1 = 0; t1 <= t; t1++) {
                    const t2 = t - t1;
                    // cut edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2] + mx[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], mx[u][t1]);
                    // keep edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], Math.max(mx[u][t1], mx[v][t2]));
                }
                dp[u] = ndp;
                mx[u] = nmx;
            }
        }
    }

    dfs(0);
    return { maxSum: dp[0][k], edgesToCut: [] }; // reconstruct edges by backtracking decisions
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Forgetting to reset mx when cutting**: When an edge is cut, the parent component's max weight remains unchanged
- **Incorrect knapsack direction**: Always iterate cuts in descending order to avoid reusing the same child multiple times
- **Off-by-one in component counting**: k edge cuts always produce k+1 components

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a fundamental DP pattern with an explicit gap constraint, a natural generalization of the classic non-adjacent selection.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index and remaining picks
- Handling fixed gap via i-g-1 transition
- Space- and time-complexity considerations
- Reconstruction of chosen indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumWithGap(A, k, g){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i-1
      if(t>0 && i-g-1>=0 && dp[i-g-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-g-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = i-g-1; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], indices: []};
}
```

